KZ3 patch 2221 (ENHANCEMENT, experimental): publish generic temporal-upscaler inputs from the live renderer.

The renderer exposes draw and scene-writeback metadata through optional callbacks. Ports own all game-specific
recognition and return only generic actions: jitter this draw, snapshot this depth, capture after this draw, or
suppress host AA before writeback. Command-list and resource handles remain opaque at the interface boundary; no
KZ3 offsets or pass rules are compiled into ps3recomp.

The selected depth is snapshotted immediately after its draw. Presentation applies scan-out gamma to the presented
surface at its own size first (as before this patch), then supplies the corrected copy, that current-frame snapshot
and jitter through rsx_upscale and safely restores resource states. A failed upscale reuses the same corrected copy,
so the shared host-AA texture keeps one size and gamma runs once per frame. Temporal
requests use bilinear output geometry and explicit bilinear fallback even when the user selected Nearest. Failed
upscaled blits fall back to the original image, and incompatible resources are never passed to CopyResource.

rsx_live_draw_set_upscale may be called from any thread: the request is written under a lock and the render thread
takes it through one interlocked serial. A sharpness change applies at once. A backend change never releases the
context from the draw path (the open command list may hold a scene capture that reads it): it waits for the next
present flush, when the frame's list has been submitted, then waits for that fence and switches. All shutdown paths
destroy the active context, and frame completion resets the interface's pre-HUD state. These hooks are shared by the port-owned FSR 3.1 adapter and a later XeSS backend.

Host coverage: tools/smoke/test_upscale_switch.py (switch timing, with mutants of the defects found in
review) and tools/smoke/test_display_geometry.py (gamma before the upscaler, fallback without a second gamma pass).

